COMPILE-FILE of ansi-test/rt.lsp intermittently HANGS or signals a spurious
type error — a GC / live-pool rooting bug.  This blocks the ANSI-test "clean
run": gclload1 does (COMPILE-AND-LOAD "rt.lsp") = COMPILE-FILE + LOAD, so the
whole suite stalls or aborts here.

SYMPTOM (non-deterministic, heap-layout dependent):
  - sometimes an infinite loop (compile-file never returns);
  - sometimes  "In check_class: TYPE ERROR: got an object of class SYMBOL,
                expected an object of class STRING".
The two faces (hang vs. reading a STRING slot as a SYMBOL) are the classic
signature of an object collected mid-use, then its memory reused.

REPRODUCE:
    sources/bocl --no-init --batch -on-error appease \
      -l ../ansi-test/rt-package.lsp \
      -x '(compile-file "/abs/path/ansi-test/rt.lsp" :output-file "/tmp/o.fasl")' \
      -x '(format t "OK~%")(finish-output)(bocl:quit 0)'
  Prints neither OK nor a clean error — it hangs, or prints the check_class
  type error and dies.

NARROWING DONE:
  - COMPILE-FILE of a *minimal* file (defmacro/defun/defstruct) works.
  - COMPILE-FILE of a byte-identical COPY of rt.lsp (e.g. /tmp/rth.lisp) often
    works where the original path hangs — i.e. it is timing/heap-layout
    dependent, NOT content- or path-specific (diff is empty; same 23695 bytes).
  - Pre-existing: present at Slice 57 (the session summary already noted the
    "check_class: expected STRING got SYMBOL" during compile-file rt.lsp).  NOT
    introduced by the hooks work (Slices 58-60); confirmed by rebuilding at
    Slice 57 and reproducing.
  - The prime suspect is the Slice-57 package-qualified symbol printing path
    (prin1 -> symbol_print_prefix -> pkg_find_symbol / find_symbol), which now
    runs per-symbol while PRIN1 writes each (large, MACROEXPAND-ALL'd) top-level
    form to the fasl.  pkg_find_symbol itself does not allocate, so the unrooted
    Ref is more likely a temporary held across an allocation elsewhere in the
    prin1 / macroexpand-all / fasl-write chain.

NEXT STEPS:
  - Build with a GC-stress mode (collect on every allocation) to make the
    corruption deterministic, then bisect which Ref is held across an alloc
    without add_to_live_pool (likely in printer.c symbol printing, or in
    %compile-file-form's prin1 of the macroexpand-all result).
  - As an interim unblock for the suite, COMPILE-AND-LOAD could fall back to a
    plain LOAD of the source (plain LOAD of rt.lsp works), or COMPILE-FILE could
    skip MACROEXPAND-ALL (write forms as-is) — but the latter exposed a separate
    PRIN1 loop on some rt.lsp form, so the rooting bug should be fixed properly.

Once fixed, re-run:
  sources/bocl --no-init --batch -on-error appease -l ../ansi-test/doit.lsp --quit
and process the remaining ANSI conformance failures (and bignums).

;; UPDATE (Slice 62): COMPILE-FILE no longer MACROEXPAND-ALL's / PRIN1's forms —
;; it is now a no-op that returns the output pathname and lets LOAD fall back to
;; the .lsp source.  So COMPILE-AND-LOAD no longer exercises the offending
;; symbol-qualified PRIN1 path, and the ANSI framework loads.  The underlying
;; GC-rooting bug in the heavy-PRIN1 path may still lurk for other heavy printing;
;; keep this open until root-caused.  (Much of last session's "hang" was actually
;; machine saturation from runaway processes + a slow per-char copy, not the GC
;; bug; the no-op COMPILE-FILE removes that slowness entirely.)
